    "C11","C12","C13","C14","C15","C48","C55","C61",
    "C104","C105","C139","C111","C120","C101","C112"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "All L1 gold-context records report absolute or systemic oral bioavailability at or above 20% for their reference molecules (paliperidone 28%, haloperidol 35%, ACT-1004-1239 53%, BI425809 71.64%, meptazinol 27.9% in cirrhosis, pethidine 0.80, pentazocine 0.68-0.70, ciramadol 80%, UDCA 78-87%).",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C13","C14","C15"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 provides no query-specific low-F evidence; the only sub-20% value is pentazocine in non-cirrhotic controls (0.18 ± 0.05), a different scaffold/condition, and does not support F < 20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C38"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 shows measurable oral exposure of the query-like 6β-naltrexol (M01) after oral naltrexone ER in humans (Cmax 17.7-64.4 pg/mL), but these are metabolite exposures without an IV comparator and do not directly quantify F.",
      "card_ids": ["C48","C55","C61"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 analog permeability/solubility supports intestinal absorption: morphine A-B Papp 4.8-8.3 × 10^-6 cm/s (C104, C105), codeine permeability higher than metoprolol (C139), morphine salt solubility 57-64.5 mg/mL (C111, C120), and naltrexone Caco-2 B-A Papp 7.8 × 10^-6 cm/s (C101, C112).",
      "card_ids": ["C104","C105","C139","C111","C120","C101","C112"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No L4 record provides direct 6β-naltrexol F or affirmative low-F evidence; B-A efflux values without an A-B comparator are not sufficient to predict F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C101","C112"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C104","C105","C139","C111","C120","C101","C112"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for 6β-naltrexol itself after oral administration.",
    "No direct query-specific Caco-2/MDCK A-B permeability or solubility; L4 uses close analogs (naltrexone, morphine, codeine) rather than the query molecule.",
    "No IV comparator for 6β-naltrexol oral exposure; L3 M01 records are metabolite exposure after oral naltrexone, not direct F.",
    "First-pass glucuronidation/sulfation of the phenol and hydroxyl groups is not quantified.",
    "B-A efflux data for naltrexone without an A-B comparator cannot quantify net absorption.",
    "Most L4 records concern unrelated molecules (estradiol, epicatechin, oxycodone, etc.) and were not used for the prediction."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high: L1 gold contexts all report F >= 20%, L2 provides no query-specific low-F evidence, L3 shows measurable oral exposure of the query-like 6β-naltrexol, and L4 analog permeability/solubility supports intestinal absorption. No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F < 20%; confidence remains low because no direct oral F for 6β-naltrexol itself is available."
}

